Using a bridge table restricts ranking to fact-driven entities, eliminating BLANK measures and simplifying DAX.
If ranking feels complex → the model is wrong, not the DAX.
Bridge tables fix models.

🎯 Goal

You want employee ranking to be as simple as product ranking
→ i.e. a one-liner RANKX, no long defensive logic.

🔴 Why Employee ranking was hard (recap in 1 line)

Because the Employee table contains people with NO sales, but ranking should only consider sellers.

So we change the model, not fight DAX.

✅ The Bridge Table Idea (Core Concept)

Instead of ranking over Employee (master list),
we rank over Employees who actually sold something.

That’s what a bridge table does.

🧩 Step 1: Create the Bridge Table

Create a new calculated table:

EmployeeSalesBridge =
SUMMARIZE(
    Sales,
    Sales[EmployeeID]
)


What this table contains:

One row per Employee who has at least one sale

No non-selling employees

Automatically clean

🧩 Step 2: Create Relationships
Employee[EmployeeID] 1 ──── * EmployeeSalesBridge[EmployeeID]
EmployeeSalesBridge[EmployeeID] 1 ──── * Sales[EmployeeID]


👉 Direction: single (default)

Now:

Visuals use EmployeeSalesBridge

Employees without sales cannot appear

🧩 Step 3: Write the SIMPLE Rank (One-liner 😄)
Rank Emp Sales =
RANKX(
    ALL ( EmployeeSalesBridge[EmployeeID] ),
    [TotalSales],
    ,
    DESC,
    DENSE
)


✅ That’s it.
No FILTER, no ISBLANK, no CALCULATE gymnastics.

The blank row appears because Power BI creates an “unknown member” when fact rows don’t match a dimension key. 
This row participates in calculations unless explicitly filtered out.

